import {texEq as eq,inlineMath} from './math.js';
import {section,table,cite} from './topic-tools.js';
const report=()=>cite('https://github.com/MoonshotAI/Kimi-K3/blob/main/k3_tech_report.pdf','Kimi K3 report');
const comparison=(caption,heads,rows)=>table(caption,heads,rows).replace('<div class="dense-table-wrap">','<div class="dense-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable Kimi K3 comparison">');
export function kimiRecipeDetails(link){return `
${section('kimi-memory','Kimi: decay the memory before correcting it',`<p>A KDA head stores a key-to-value matrix instead of a list of past tokens. Its query and key have width dₖ; its value has width dᵥ. Short convolutions and Swish precede query/key L2 normalization. The write strength β is a sigmoid output. Forgetting acts separately on each key feature.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}\bar S_t&=\operatorname{Diag}(\alpha_t)S_{t-1},\qquad S_t\in\mathbb R^{d_k\times d_v}\\r_t&=v_t-\bar S_t^\top k_t\\S_t&=\bar S_t+\beta_t k_t r_t^\top\\o_t&=S_t^\top q_t\end{aligned}`,'q and k are dₖ-vectors; v and r are dᵥ-vectors. Predict from the decayed state, then write the residual, then read the updated state. This expands report Eq. 1.')}
<p>The operation order matters. With a scalar state of 2, decay 0.5, key 1, value 3 and ${inlineMath(String.raw`\beta=1`)}, decay gives 1. The correction is ${inlineMath(String.raw`3-1=2`)}, so the final state is 3. Predicting from the old state would give 2 instead.</p>
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="KDA operation order: decay old memory; predict the current value; write the prediction error; read updated memory"><div>Old memory</div><span>→</span><div>Feature-wise decay</div><span>→</span><div>Predict value<br>Compute error</div><span>→</span><div>Rank-one write</div><span>→</span><div>Query read</div></div>
<p>K3 bounds log decay to protect the reciprocal cumulative factors used by its chunked kernel. Its learned decay logits use a low-rank projection and head bias. The separate per-head A starts at zero.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}g_t&=-5\,\sigma(e^{A_{\rm head}}z_t),\qquad\alpha_t=e^{g_t}\\-5&<g_{t,j}<0,\qquad e^{-5}<\alpha_{t,j}<1\\-80&<\sum_{u=1}^{16}g_{u,j}<0,\qquad\left(\prod_{u=1}^{16}\alpha_{u,j}\right)^{-1}<e^{80}\end{aligned}`,'The strict bounds describe finite analytic inputs, not every rounded machine value. The cumulative bound is for a 16-position tile; it does not bound an arbitrarily long product.')}
<p>The tile bound keeps reciprocal scaling within BF16 dynamic range. It permits a Tensor Core diagonal-tile path rather than expensive position-pair calculations. BF16 representability does not remove rounding error. The chunked causal lower triangle includes its diagonal because the read follows the current write.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}y_{\rm KDA}&=W_o\!\left[\sigma(W_gx)\odot\operatorname{RMSNorm}(o)\right]\\y_{\rm MLA}&=W_o\!\left[\sigma(W_gx)\odot o\right]\end{aligned}`,'These are the report’s output-gate forms, Eqs. 6–7. Both gates are full rank. The extra output RMSNorm is shown for KDA, not silently added to MLA.')}
<p>The fixed matrix compresses history. Twenty-four gated MLA layers still retrieve particular earlier tokens. NoPE removes the need to rescale rotary positions, but it does not guarantee perfect long-context retrieval. <a href="${link('models/attention')}#delta">Attention →</a> owns the broader recurrent/softmax comparison. ${report()} §2.1.</p>`)}
${section('kimi-depth','Kimi: choose representations across depth',`<p>Ordinary residual addition merges earlier updates into one accumulated state. Attention Residuals instead choose among earlier updates. A learned query belongs to the receiving layer; the keys are normalized source representations. The values retain their original scale.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}a_{\ell i}&=\frac{\exp(w_\ell^\top\operatorname{RMSNorm}(v_i))}{\sum_{j<\ell}\exp(w_\ell^\top\operatorname{RMSNorm}(v_j))}\\r_\ell&=\sum_{i<\ell}a_{\ell i}v_i\end{aligned}`,'Sources include the embedding and preceding layer updates. RMSNorm applies to keys in the score, not to the value being mixed. This is depth attention, not token attention.')}
<p>Full depth attention repeatedly reads an increasing number of sources. K3 sums updates within each block, then attends over completed block summaries and the current partial block. The current partial source is absent at the first layer of a block. The embedding remains available.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}\mathrm{full\ depth\ arithmetic}&=O(L^2d)\\\mathrm{full\ source\ storage}&=O(Ld)\\\mathrm{block\ source\ storage}&=O(Nd)\end{aligned}`,'L is depth, N is block count and d is model width. Storage is per token for residual sources, not the complete training activation budget.')}
<p>The primary report explicitly specifies eight blocks with block size twelve and a partial final block, plus the embedding: nine sources in total. This is not a claim that the decoder contains 96 full layers. Learned depth selection and compressed sequence memory solve different problems.</p>
<p>The MoE then provides selective parameter access. Its routed path uses width 3,584, RMSNorm before the up-projection and bounded SiTU-GLU with ${inlineMath(String.raw`\beta_1=4,\ \beta_2=25`)}. Two shared experts retain full width. Quantile Balancing computes biases from score-margin quantiles, rather than a fixed-size signed correction. Narrowing the path alone does not establish stability.</p>
<p><a href="${link('models/transformers')}#block-attnres">Block Attention Residuals →</a> explains the summaries. <a href="${link('models/moe')}#latent-experts">LatentMoE →</a> contains the projection flow and exact activation formula. <a href="${link('models/moe')}#quantile-balancing">Quantile Balancing →</a> follows the controller. ${report()} §§2.2–2.3.</p>`)}
${section('kimi-training','Kimi: connect visual inputs, data and task dependencies',`<p>The architecture needs a data recipe that exercises its memory and selective capacity. MoonViT-V2 has about 401M parameters and 27 layers. It trains jointly from scratch with next-token prediction, rather than starting from a contrastive SigLIP checkpoint. Images and video share parameters. The encoder uses 14×14 patches and factorized spatial/temporal attention. A 2×2 pixel shuffle reduces visual tokens fourfold; maximum image resolution is 3,584×3,584.</p>
<p>The authors report matching their SigLIP-initialized baseline with lower gradient norms and fewer spikes. This result does not establish that contrastive initialization is always unnecessary. The mixture includes natural and interleaved images, OCR, perception, video, visual code and rendered SVG, web, game, 3D and CAD artifacts. These sources exercise interfaces and documents as well as natural-image recognition.</p>
<p>Text pretraining combines web, code, mathematics and knowledge. Fidelity-checked rewrites vary style and perspective. The team tunes cosine and Warmup-Stable-Decay schedules independently before comparison; cosine wins in that setting. Sharing one hyperparameter choice can measure tuning bias rather than schedule quality.</p>
<p>Context grows 8K→64K in main training, then 256K→1M during cooldown. Concatenated and permuted multimodal documents and subtasks create dependencies across the sequence. Presenting a long input alone does not ensure that solving it needs distant evidence.</p>
<p>Agent training extends that principle from distant evidence to actions. A self-expanding hierarchical knowledge graph supplies retrieved material, increasingly fine concepts and combinations of related nodes. Autonomous Execution Tasks define initial state, goal, tools, budget and verifier, without a reference trajectory. Configurable harness modules vary system prompts, tools, context management, skills, memory and subagents. Public diagnostics guide recovery; hidden final verification evaluates completion.</p>
<p>This gives specialists tasks that require decomposition, execution and recovery, rather than imitation of one tool sequence. The next section connects those tasks to rewards and consolidation. ${report()} §§2.4,3–4.2.</p>`)}
${section('kimi-teachers','Kimi: consolidate specialists on student prefixes',`<p>The recipe first trains SFT, then domain/effort specialists, then consolidates them. Specialist families cover general reasoning and vision, search and knowledge, long-horizon agents, and coding. Nine teachers do not imply every Cartesian combination of family and effort.</p>
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="Multi-teacher on-policy distillation: select domain and effort; student samples a trajectory; matching teacher scores the same prefix; combine dense teacher signal and task reward"><div>Select domain<br>and effort</div><span>→</span><div>Student samples<br>its own prefix</div><span>→</span><div>Matching teacher<br>scores sampled token</div><span>→</span><div>Dense feedback<br>+ task reward</div></div>
${eq(String.raw`r_t^d=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(\operatorname{sg}\!\left[\log\frac{\pi_T^{(d,e)}(y_t\mid x,y_{<t})}{\pi_\theta(y_t\mid e,x,y_{<t})}\right],-R_{\max},R_{\max}\right)`,'d selects the domain; e selects low, high or max effort. sg stops gradients through the feedback. Rmax is positive; this summary does not invent its undisclosed value.')}
<p>The student receives guidance on contexts it actually visits. The system need not generate a large fixed dataset of teacher trajectories for this stage. Specialists can improve independently before consolidation. The report found no clear benefit from its tested top-k distillation objectives; that is a setting-specific observation.</p>
${eq(String.raw`R(y)=\begin{cases}-1,&T(y)>\tau b_0(x)\\R_{\rm task}(y),&T(y)\le\tau b_0(x)\end{cases}`,'b₀(x) is the problem’s cold-start token budget. General tasks count thinking tokens; agents count cumulative model output, including reasoning and tool arguments. This replaces task reward on overrun, rather than subtracting one.')}
<p>Training begins with a large but capped max-effort budget. The team lowers τ to obtain high- and low-effort teachers, with domain-specific human guidance. This training consequence is neither a soft length penalty nor an inference hard-token cap.</p>
<p>Non-verifiable tasks also use an agentic generative reward model. Its judge reads the artifact, creates a rubric, scores candidates and records scores before tournament-style binary comparisons. A candidate beyond its cold-start verbosity multiplier automatically loses. This output-verbosity rule is distinct from the reasoning budget.</p>
<p>Partial rollouts address stragglers. Generation pauses when a fraction of the N×K trajectories finishes. Unfinished runs are prioritized next iteration. A prompt group reaches optimization only once all K responses complete. Resumption can cross policy versions; per-token regularization keeps updates local, but does not make stale tokens on-policy.</p>
<p><a href="${link('post-training/distillation')}">Distillation →</a>, <a href="${link('post-training/reasoning')}">Reasoning Effort →</a> and <a href="${link('post-training/agents')}">Agent Training →</a> own the reusable mechanisms. ${report()} §§4.1.2–4.1.3.</p>`)}
${section('kimi-evidence','Kimi: align the deployment objective and read the scores',`<p>Deployment-aware post-training uses MXFP4 routed expert weights and MXFP8 expert activations throughout SFT and RL. Attention, latent projections, shared experts and routers retain higher precision. Matching quantization reduces one training/rollout mismatch; it does not prove every runtime operation is identical.</p>
<p>A pretrained MTP layer becomes a one-layer EAGLE-3 drafter. The target stays frozen; only the draft layer and its feature-fusion projection train. The drafter joins outputs from the first, fourth and final AttnRes blocks. Its bias-free fusion matrix starts as [0 0 I], so initialization keeps the high-level feature. Seven training unrolls feed earlier draft outputs after the first step.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\mathcal L_{\rm LK}=-\log\sum_{x\in\mathcal V}\min\{p(x),q(x)\}`,'p is the target next-token distribution and q is the draft distribution. K3 evaluates both at temperature 1, with no auxiliary ground-truth cross-entropy term.')}
<p>Distribution overlap is the expected single-token acceptance under lossless speculative sampling. It is not itself throughput: proposal work, verification and state repair still cost time. KDA cannot remove a rejected suffix by slicing its matrix. K3 caches projected draft inputs and replays only the accepted prefix. <a href="${link('execution/speculation')}">Speculative Decoding →</a> retains acceptance and cache-correctness conditions.</p>
${comparison('Selected Kimi K3 results; all use max effort and temperature 1',['Benchmark / condition','Reported result'],[['GPQA Diamond','93.5'],['BrowseComp','91.2'],['DeepSearchQA, F1','95.0'],['Terminal-Bench 2.1','88.3'],['FrontierSWE','81.2'],['Math-Vision, without / with Python','94.3 / 97.8'],['WebDev Arena, reported snapshot','1,678 Elo; rank 1/99']])}
<p>Single-step evaluations use top-p 0.95; agentic evaluations use 1.0. Terminal-Bench reports the best score across harnesses for each model. BrowseComp uses compaction at 300K tokens; without context management over the full 1M window, K3 scores 90.4 instead. Vision scores generally average three runs. Tools and harnesses are part of these results, not cosmetic settings.</p>
<p>Some tasks use model judges or internal environments. External leaderboard results are dated snapshots. The combined recipe changes architecture, data, scale, RL and systems, so these scores cannot isolate one component’s contribution. The reported 2.5× scaling efficiency is likewise a complete-recipe comparison, not a measured 2.5× serving speedup.</p>
<p>FlashKDA handles the recurrent operator; associative context parallelism joins segment transitions. MoonEP uses redundant experts and routing plans. Recomputation, FP8 activation storage and remote offload reduce different memory terms. Checkpointable AgentENV microVMs and the CPU KV pool let waiting trajectories release active resources. Its reported 51M sandboxes and 1.5M images are accumulated infrastructure counts, not unique tasks or simultaneous concurrency.</p>
<p>The systems follow the model’s dependencies: fixed recurrent state, growing global KV, distributed expert weights and suspended environments. <a href="${link('training/parallelism')}">Distributed Training →</a> and <a href="${link('execution/serving')}">Serving →</a> explain these separate costs. ${report()} §§4.1.4–6 · ${cite('https://www.kimi.com/blog/kimi-k3','Kimi release explanation')} · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3','Released weights')}</p>`)}
`;}
